+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"agent_message","text":"Done. The constraint-based event scheduler is complete — 32 tests pass, `tsc` builds clean, `eslint` is happy.\n\n**Files**\n- `src/types.ts` — `Event`, `Room`, `ScheduleOptions`, `Assignment`, `ScheduleResult`\n- `src/scheduler.ts:171` — `schedule()` entry and engine\n- `src/index.ts` — named + default `schedule` export, `Scheduler` class, `createScheduler()` factory\n- `tests/scheduler.test.ts` — 32 tests across all seven scheduling rules\n\n**How it works**\n- **Validation**: throws on inverted windows, non-positive durations, negative gaps, duplicate event IDs, unknown prerequisites, and cycles (Kahn's topological sort detects self-references and multi-event cycles, naming the involved events).\n- **Preprocessing**: computes each event's earliest possible start along its prerequisite chain and builds per-event room lists — capacity-eligible rooms sorted smallest-first, with the preferred room tried first.\n- **Phase 1 greedy**: repeatedly places the *most constrained* available event (least slack between its earliest allowed time and its best feasible start), rather than naive input order — this alone handles most contention.\n- **Phase 3 search**: if greedy leaves schedulable events behind, a bounded branch-and-bound DFS explores per-room placement choices and skip-branches, pruning with a remaining-potential bound and a time budget scaled to problem size (2s base + 250ms/event, capped at 10s).\n- **Compaction**: final re-placement pass minimizes end times (ties prefer the preferred room) so schedules come out tight.\n\n**Verification beyond the suite**\n- 60 randomized small cases matched a brute-force optimum enumerator exactly.\n- 40 randomized large cases (12–30 events) all produced fully valid schedules; median runtime 0ms, worst ~9s only for hard 30-event instances where the search is still improving on greedy.\n- Adversarial case confirmed: a 240-min event blocking three 150-min events is correctly skipped (3 scheduled vs. 2 for first-fit).\n\nOne judgment call worth noting: `preferredRoom` is honored as a soft hint per the spec — the scheduler falls back to other rooms when the preferred one is full, occupied, or too small, rather than leaving the event unscheduled."}}
